<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Siak Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1723 CE – 1946 CE · capital Siak Sri Indrapura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Riau sultanate founded by the adventurer Raja Kecil, once master of the east Sumatran coast and later a model of royal patriotism.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-kecil">Raja Kecil</a>, a Minangkabau-backed claimant who said he was the posthumous son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Mahmud II of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor</a>, seized the Johor Sultanate in 1718. Driven out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> allies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> nobility in 1722, he withdrew to the Siak river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> and founded a new sultanate at Buantan in 1723.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Siak controlled the trade of the Siak river, exporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, gold and forest products, and in the 18th century extended its overlordship over the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/deli-sultanate">Deli Sultanate</a> and other coastal states. Arab-descended sultans of the Syarif line ruled from 1791. The court moved to Siak Sri Indrapura, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siak-sri-indrapura-palace">Asserayah Hasyimiah</a> palace was built in 1889.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A treaty of 1858 made Siak a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> protected state. In 1945 its last sultan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syarif-kasim-ii">Syarif Kasim II</a>, declared support for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Republic of Indonesia</a> and gave it his treasury; the sultanate was merged into the republic in 1946, and he was named a national hero.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/siak-sultanate.jpg" alt="Siak Sultanate" width="320" height="211"><figcaption>Image: Wakanebe Wizard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sultanate_of_Siak_Sri_Indrapura.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1723 CE – 1946 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Siak Sri Indrapura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Raja Kecil, Syarif Kasim II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Indonesia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Siak Sri Indrapura, Sultanate of Siak</dd></dl></aside></div>
